          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Green-Tao Theorem states that there are arithmetic sequences of prime numbers of any finite length.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p6-q17-task",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Prove that there is no infinite arithmetic sequence of prime numbers with a nonzero common difference.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No such sequence exists.\n\nIf its first term is the prime $p$ and its common difference is $d>0$, then $a_{p+1}=p(1+d)$ is composite.",
      "worked": "Suppose such a sequence has first term $p$ and nonzero common difference $d$. Since its terms are positive integers, $d$ is an integer. A negative common difference eventually gives a nonpositive term, so $d>0$.\n\nUsing the arithmetic-sequence formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}a_{p+1}&=p+((p+1)-1)d\\\\a_{p+1}&=p+pd\\\\a_{p+1}&=p(1+d).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth $p$ and $1+d$ are integers greater than $1$, so $a_{p+1}$ is composite. This contradicts the assumption that every term is prime. Therefore, no infinite arithmetic sequence of primes has a nonzero common difference.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
